# Day 08 — lab/terraform/.gitignore
#
# Same hygiene as Days 05, 06 and 07, with a Day 08 addition at the bottom.

# STATE. Plaintext JSON listing every resource this stack has touched — and on
# this day, when create_rds is true, containing the database master password in
# the clear. Day 05 made the argument; nothing about it has changed.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfstate.backup
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

# Provider cache. Hundreds of MB, machine-specific, regenerated by init.
.terraform/

# Saved plans can contain sensitive values in the clear.
*.tfplan
tfplan
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Real variable values. The .example file IS the documentation and IS committed.
terraform.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars
!terraform.tfvars.example

# Lambda zips built by the archive provider at plan time.
build/
*.zip

# ==========================================================================
# DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED: .terraform.lock.hcl
# ==========================================================================
# Commit it. It is what makes `terraform init` produce the same provider
# binary on your laptop, your colleague's laptop and in CI. Day 05's IAC-012
# is the check that catches a repo which ignores it.
#
# It matters slightly more here than elsewhere. This is the first day with two
# provider configurations, and a lock file is what stops the DR region being
# planned by a different provider version than the primary — which is a class
# of drift that only manifests when you use the DR region, which is during an
# incident.

# ==========================================================================
# DAY 08 ADDITION: RECOVERY EVIDENCE AND TIMING ARTEFACTS
# ==========================================================================
#
# Three kinds of file this lab produces that should not reach a repository,
# and one that should.
#
# FAILOVER AND RESTORE OUTPUT. `describe-db-snapshots`, `describe-snapshots`,
# `list-backup-jobs` and friends emit your account id, your resource
# inventory, your AZ layout and your backup schedule. A backup schedule is
# operationally sensitive in a way people underrate: it tells an attacker
# exactly when your last recoverable point is, which tells them how long they
# have before a restore becomes cheap.
#
# DYNAMODB AND RDS EXPORTS taken during a restore drill are production data
# with a different filename.
#
# CHAOS INVOCATION RESPONSES contain instance ids, subnet ids and NACL
# association ids — a map of what to break and how.
#
# WHAT SHOULD BE COMMITTED, and is not ignored: your measured RTO and RPO.
# rto-measurements.md is the single most valuable artefact this day produces.
# Write the numbers down, commit them, and date them, because an RTO without a
# date is an RTO from an architecture that no longer exists.
findings.json
findings-*.json
findings.csv
dr-audit-*.json
restore-*.json
snapshots-*.json
backup-jobs-*.json
chaos-*.json
failover-evidence/
*.dump
*.sql.gz
